# Cloudflare Pages reads this file only at the top of the site, so the app's own rules
# (apps/web/public/_headers) are restated here under /read/. The release leaves the app's
# copy out. Hashed build files never change; everything else revalidates so updates land.
# Cloudflare serves a page at its address without .html (privacy.html at /privacy, the app's
# index.html at /read/), so the rules name those addresses.

# Every address, the app's included: another site may not put a page in a frame, and the
# browser may not guess a file's type. A link to another site carries this site's name only,
# never the page (the confirm and leave pages, which are made by the server code, send none
# at all). No rule that keeps other tabs away (the "opener" one): the release refuses it, as it would cut the old
# address's tab off from this one and readers could not carry their marks across.
/*
  X-Frame-Options: DENY
  X-Content-Type-Options: nosniff
  Referrer-Policy: strict-origin-when-cross-origin

# The front pages name every place a script, frame, picture or font may come from: this site,
# and Cloudflare's spam check (its script, and the frame it draws the check in). Nothing is
# written into the pages themselves, so nothing needs 'unsafe-inline'. The "no page here" page
# is served at every unknown address, so it has only the rule above. The app's own rule is at
# the end, under /read/.
/
  Cache-Control: no-cache
  Content-Security-Policy: default-src 'self'; script-src 'self' https://challenges.cloudflare.com; frame-src https://challenges.cloudflare.com; connect-src 'self'; img-src 'self'; style-src 'self'; font-src 'self'; form-action 'self'; base-uri 'none'; object-src 'none'; frame-ancestors 'none'

